---
import '@fontsource/spectral/300.css';
import '@fontsource/spectral/300-italic.css';
import '@fontsource/spectral/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { bodies } from '../data/bodies';

// Every photograph on the site, read straight from the pages so this list can never fall out of step
const pages = import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { query: '?raw', import: 'default', eager: true }) as Record<string, string>;
const plates = bodies.flatMap((b) => {
  const src = pages[`./${b.slug}.astro`];
  if (!src) return [];
  const out: { world: string; slug: string; img: string; alt: string; caption: string }[] = [];
  const re = /<img src="(\/img\/[^"]+)"[^>]*?alt="([^"]*)"[\s\S]*?<figcaption>([\s\S]*?)<\/figcaption>/g;
  for (const m of src.matchAll(re)) out.push({ world: b.slug === 'sun' ? 'The Sun' : b.name, slug: b.slug, img: m[1], alt: m[2], caption: m[3].replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '').trim() });
  return out;
});
const factSheets = bodies.map((b) => ({ name: b.slug === 'sun' ? 'The Sun' : b.name, url: `https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/${b.slug}fact.html` }));
import { portraits } from '../lib/portraits';
import { HYG, MCCONNACHIE, TWO_MRS, WMAP, places, D3_CELESTIAL } from '../lib/v2/atlas/places';
---
<Base title="Sources" path="/sources" description="Where every number, map and photograph on Heliocodex comes from, and which parts are drawn rather than photographed.">
  <article class="ledger">
    <header class="ledger-head">
      <p class="ledger-kicker">Sources</p>
      <h1>Where it all comes from</h1>
      <p class="ledger-lead">Every number, map and photograph here, and who made it. The words are written for this site. Where something is drawn by the computer rather than measured or photographed, it says so below. If you find a mistake, it is ours, not theirs.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="entry">
      <h2>Numbers</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>Sizes, masses, densities, gravity, temperatures, the length of each day and year, and the moon counts come from the <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/">NASA Space Science Data Coordinated Archive fact sheets</a>, one for each body.</p>
        <ul class="sheets">
          {factSheets.map((f) => <li><a href={f.url}>{f.name}</a></li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>Moon counts change as new moons are confirmed, so they are only as current as NASA’s sheet.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <h2>Where the planets are</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The model on the <a href="/bodies">Bodies page</a> places each planet from the mean orbital elements in <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html">Keplerian Elements for Approximate Positions of the Major Planets</a> (NASA/JPL Solar System Dynamics, Table 1, good from 1800 to 2050). That puts each planet within a fraction of a degree of where it really is, plenty for a picture, though it is not a full ephemeris.</p>
        <p>Where a page says where the Sun stands in another world’s sky, the angle comes from <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/">JPL Horizons</a>: the sub-solar latitude on 9 November 2026 was 74.3° north on Uranus and 19.5° south on Neptune.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <h2>Maps of the surfaces</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The global colour maps wrapped round each world are from <a href="https://www.solarsystemscope.com/textures/">Solar System Scope</a>, under a <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a> licence. They are built from NASA mission imagery: MESSENGER for Mercury, Viking and later orbiters for Mars, Cassini for Jupiter and Saturn, and NASA’s Blue Marble and Black Marble for the Earth by day and night. Heliocodex only resizes them to 4k and 8k and saves them smaller, with Mercury and the Earth’s clouds in greyscale.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <h2>The stars</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The 9,000 or so stars behind every scene are the <a href="https://heasarc.gsfc.nasa.gov/w3browse/star-catalog/bsc5p.html">Yale Bright Star Catalogue</a> (5th revised edition, Hoffleit and Warren, via NASA HEASARC): every star you could see from a dark site on Earth, placed where it really is, sized by brightness and coloured by its measured colour index.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry" id="portraits">
      <h2>Portraits of the planets</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The photographs of the Sun and planets on the home page and the planets page, each made smaller for the web:</p>
        <ul>
          {portraits.map((p) => <li><span>{p.name}</span> {p.credit}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry" id="videos">
      <h2>Videos</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>A few lessons carry a NASA video, which plays from YouTube only when its Play button is pressed. The still shown before that is kept on this site. They are <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14576">a flight into a black hole</a> (NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center/J. Schnittman and B. Powell), the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l3QQQu7QLoM">active Sun</a> (NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center/SDO) and the <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/4619">tour of the Moon</a> (NASA’s Scientific Visualization Studio). NASA does not endorse Heliocodex.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry" id="photographs">
      <h2>Photographs in the lessons</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>Where a real picture of something exists, the lessons show it and say so beside it. The black holes in M87 and at the centre of our galaxy are by the <a href="https://eventhorizontelescope.org/">Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration</a>, from <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1907a/">ESO</a> (<a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2208-eht-mwa/">and here</a>). The jet of M87 is by <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo0020a/">The Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA) and NASA/ESA</a>. All three are shared under a <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a> licence. They have been made smaller for the web, and the first is cropped square.</p>
        <p>From the Hubble Space Telescope, by way of <a href="https://esahubble.org/">ESA/Hubble</a>: the star cluster <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0910g/">Omega Centauri</a> (NASA, ESA and the Hubble SM4 ERO Team), the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0601a/">Orion Nebula</a> (NASA, ESA, M. Robberto and the Hubble Space Telescope Orion Treasury Project Team), the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1310a/">Ring Nebula</a> (NASA, ESA and C. Robert O’Dell), the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0515a/">Crab Nebula</a> (NASA, ESA, J. Hester and A. Loll), the pulsing star <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1323a/">RS Puppis</a> (NASA, ESA and the Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA)-Hubble/Europe Collaboration; acknowledgment H. Bond), the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1717a/">glow of two neutron stars colliding in NGC 4993</a> (NASA and ESA; acknowledgment A.J. Levan, N.R. Tanvir, A. Fruchter and O. Fox) and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000273">heart of the Crab</a> (NASA and ESA; J. Hester and M. Weisskopf). The <a href="https://esawebb.org/images/weic2216b/">Pillars of Creation</a> are from the James Webb Space Telescope (NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI; J. DePasquale, A. Koekemoer, A. Pagan). The face of <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1726a/">Antares</a> is by ESO/K. Ohnaka, and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2105a/">second picture of M87’s black hole</a> is by the EHT Collaboration. Also from Hubble are <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo9919i/">Supernova 1994D</a> (NASA/ESA, The Hubble Key Project Team and The High-Z Supernova Search Team) and <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/potw1142a/">Supernova 1987A</a> (ESA/Hubble and NASA), and from Webb <a href="https://esawebb.org/images/weic2330a/">Cassiopeia A</a> (NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, D. Milisavljevic, T. Temim, I. De Looze). ESA/Hubble, ESA/Webb and ESO share their pictures under the same Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence.</p>
        <p>The portrait of <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einstein_patentoffice.jpg">Albert Einstein in Bern</a>, taken in 1904 or 1905 by Lucien Chavan, is in the public domain and comes by way of Wikimedia Commons.</p>
        <p>The photograph of the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1926a/">total eclipse of 29 May 1919</a>, restored from a copy of one of the original plates, is credited to ESO/Landessternwarte Heidelberg-Königstuhl/F. W. Dyson, A. S. Eddington and C. Davidson and used under CC BY 4.0. The circles and star names on it were added by ESO.</p>
        <p>The Hubble photographs of the galaxy cluster <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1711a/">Abell 370</a> (NASA, ESA/Hubble, HST Frontier Fields), the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/potw1151a/">Cosmic Horseshoe</a> and the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/potw1204a/">Einstein Cross</a> (both ESA/Hubble and NASA) are used under CC BY 4.0. The last two are cropped.</p>
        <p>The photograph of the <a href="https://www.usgs.gov/media/images/icecube-lab-south-pole">IceCube Laboratory at the South Pole</a> is from the US Geological Survey and is in the public domain.</p>
        <p>The infrared picture of the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0515a/">brown dwarf 2M1207 and its planet</a> is credited to ESO and used under CC BY 4.0.</p>
        <p>From NASA, in the lesson on the habitable zone: <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA23791">Venus from Mariner 10</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech), the <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA00407">Viking mosaic of Mars</a> (NASA/JPL/USGS) and the <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA11688">jets of Enceladus</a> (NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute).</p>
        <p>The two pictures of the dark cloud <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso0102/">Barnard 68</a>, in visible light and with infrared added, are credited to ESO and used under CC BY 4.0.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on spiral arms: the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0506a/">Whirlpool Galaxy</a> (NASA, ESA, S. Beckwith and the Hubble Heritage Team) and <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo0501a/">NGC 1300</a> (NASA, ESA and the Hubble Heritage Team) from Hubble, and <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1118a/">NGC 6744</a> from ESO, all under CC BY 4.0.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on the oldest light: the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/wmap/wmap-overview/">nine-year map of the microwave sky</a> (NASA/WMAP Science Team) and the 1962 photograph of the <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horn_Antenna-in_Holmdel,_New_Jersey.jpeg">horn antenna at Holmdel</a> (NASA, GPN-2003-00013). NASA places no copyright on either.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on galaxy clusters: the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0813a/">Coma Cluster</a> from Hubble (NASA, ESA and the Hubble Heritage Team, with thanks to D. Carter and the Coma HST ACS Treasury Team, CC BY 4.0) and the X-ray picture of the <a href="https://chandra.harvard.edu/photo/2006/1e0657/">Bullet Cluster</a> from the Chandra X-ray Observatory (NASA/CXC/CfA/M. Markevitch et al.).</p>
        <p>In the lesson on the inside of planets: <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1613a/">Jupiter and the lights at its pole</a> from Hubble (NASA, ESA, CC BY 4.0), cut down in size.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on falling into a black hole: a frame from the <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/13326">black hole and disc simulation</a> of 2019 (NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center/Jeremy Schnittman), cut down in size. It is a computer simulation, not a photograph.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on how galaxies form and change: <a href="https://esawebb.org/images/jades4/">JADES-GS-z14-0</a> from Webb (NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, B. Robertson, B. Johnson, S. Tacchella, P. Cargile, CC BY 4.0) and the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1315a/">Hubble sequence at three ages</a> (NASA, ESA, M. Kornmesser, CC BY 4.0). Both were cut down in size.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on how it all ends: the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic2501a/">Andromeda galaxy</a> from Hubble (NASA, ESA, B. Williams, University of Washington, CC BY 4.0), cut down in size.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on what life needs: the <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_prismatic_spring.jpg">Grand Prismatic Spring</a> (Jim Peaco, US National Park Service, public domain), a <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blacksmoker_in_Atlantic_Ocean.jpg">black smoker in the Atlantic</a> (P. Rona, OAR/National Undersea Research Program, NOAA, public domain) and a <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SEM_image_of_Milnesium_tardigradum_in_active_state_-_journal.pone.0045682.g001-2.png">water bear under an electron microscope</a> (Schokraie and others 2012, PLoS ONE 7(9): e45682, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5/">CC BY 2.5</a>, lightened and turned round by Wikimedia Commons user Epipelagic). All three were cut down in size. The picture at the top of the brown dwarfs lesson is the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14721">WISE view of the brown dwarf WISE 1828+2650</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/UCLA).</p>
        <p>In the lesson on looking for life in the Solar System: the rock <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA26368">Cheyava Falls</a> on Mars (NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS), <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19048">Europa</a> from Galileo (NASA/JPL-Caltech/SETI Institute, turned so that north is up) and the <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA07232">surface of Titan</a> from Huygens (NASA/JPL/ESA/University of Arizona, with its white border trimmed). The first two were cut down in size.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on listening for someone else: the star cluster <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo0840a/">M13</a> from Hubble (NASA, ESA and the Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA), CC BY 4.0, cut down in size) and <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mementos-of-earth">the Golden Record at Cape Canaveral in 1977</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech).</p>
        <p>In the four lessons on exploring space, all from NASA, which places no copyright on its own pictures: <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/9132833">Robert Goddard and his rocket of 1926</a>, the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/6901001">launch of Apollo 11</a>, a <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ20261001_admin_0012">Falcon 9 booster landing</a> (NASA/John Kraus), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/KSC-20260401-PH-SFL01_0029">boosters of Artemis II falling away</a>, the <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sputnik_asm.jpg">replica of Sputnik 1</a> (NASA/NSSDC), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/as17-140-21496">Harrison Schmitt on the Moon</a>, <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/art002e021278">Earthset from Artemis II</a>, <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA01492">Neptune from Voyager 2</a> (NASA/JPL), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24428">Perseverance rover landing</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22207">Curiosity’s picture of itself</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA17172">Saturn from behind</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/SSI), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/jsc2024e023024">dust from the asteroid Bennu</a> (NASA/Erika Blumenfeld and Joseph Aebersold), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss024e007998">Shannon Walker on the treadmill</a>, <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss058e003901">David Saint-Jacques in the cupola</a> and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/S84-27031">Bruce McCandless flying free</a>. All have been made smaller for the web and some are cropped. The film of the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4czjS9h4Fpg">Perseverance landing</a> is NASA/JPL-Caltech’s own.</p>
        <p>In the lessons on how we found out: the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/MAF_20250314_LunarEclipse_composite">lunar eclipse of March 2025</a> (NASA/Eric Bordelon) and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e001729">Venus crossing the Sun in 2012</a> (NASA/SDO, HMI), both from NASA; and four pictures in the public domain by way of Wikimedia Commons: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sidereus_Nuncius_Medicean_Stars.jpg">a page</a> and <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galileo%27s_sketches_of_the_moon.png">the Moon drawings</a> from Galileo’s book of 1610, the <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copernican_heliocentrism_diagram-2.jpg">diagram from Copernicus’s book of 1543</a>, and the <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leavitt_aavso.jpg">portrait of Henrietta Leavitt</a> of about 1898. The <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_resolution_Au100.JPG">microscope picture of gold atoms</a> in the lesson on atoms is by Erwin Rossen, who released it into the public domain.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on telescopes in space: <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/s125e012033">Hubble in orbit in 2009</a> (NASA) and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000188">Webb’s mirror</a> (NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center), both from NASA and cut down in size; the galaxy <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo9401a/">M100 before and after Hubble’s repair</a> (NASA, STScI), with its two pictures cut out of the original sheet and set side by side; the <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1501a/">Pillars of Creation from Hubble</a> (NASA, ESA, Hubble Heritage Team, CC BY 4.0), trimmed and set beside the Webb picture credited above; and <a href="https://esawebb.org/images/weic2209a/">Webb’s first deep field</a> (NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, CC BY 4.0).</p>
        <p>In the lessons on red dwarfs and brown dwarfs: <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/potw1343a/">Proxima Centauri</a> from Hubble (ESA/Hubble and NASA, CC BY 4.0); the two <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo9548a/">discovery pictures of Gliese 229B</a> (T. Nakajima and S. Kulkarni of Caltech, S. Durrance and D. Golimowski of JHU, and NASA), cut out of the original sheet and set side by side without its lettering; and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1404b/">weather map of Luhman 16B</a> (ESO/I. Crossfield, CC BY 4.0), which is a map worked out from measurements and not a photograph. All three were cut down in size. The picture at the top of the brown dwarfs lesson is the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14721">WISE view of the brown dwarf WISE 1828+2650</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/UCLA).</p>
        <p>In the lesson on giants and supergiants: the red supergiant <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2417a/">WOH G64</a> (ESO/K. Ohnaka et al., CC BY 4.0), cut down in size.</p>
        <p>In the lesson on magnetars and gamma-ray bursts: the <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14227">X-ray rings of the burst GRB 221009A</a> (NASA/Swift/A. Beardmore, University of Leicester) and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10711">ring round the magnetar SGR 1900+14</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech).</p>
        <p>In the lesson on whether black holes last for ever: <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/200804210009HQ">Stephen Hawking in 2008</a> (NASA/Paul E. Alers) and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e002215">sky in gamma rays</a> (NASA/DOE/Fermi LAT Collaboration), from which the mission’s badge in one corner was painted out. Both were cut down in size.</p>
        <p>Added to the older lessons on the sky and the stars: <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1347a/">Orion above the ALMA telescopes</a> (ESO/B. Tafreshi, twanight.org, CC BY 4.0) and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/b11/">Pleiades</a> (ESO/S. Brunier, CC BY 4.0); the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/sts075-351-022">Southern Cross from the shuttle Columbia</a> (NASA), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ201708210100">total eclipse of the Sun of 2017</a> (NASA/Aubrey Gemignani) and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e001175">Earth at the solstices and equinoxes</a> (NASA Earth Observatory, with data from EUMETSAT); and <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/potw1635a/">Alpha Centauri A and B</a> (ESA/Hubble and NASA, CC BY 4.0) and <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0516a/">Sirius A and B</a> (NASA, ESA, H. Bond of STScI and M. Barstow of the University of Leicester, CC BY 4.0) from Hubble. All were cut down in size. The charts in these lessons were drawn for this site.</p>
        <p>Added to the older lessons on the Sun, the planets and the moons: <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/phot-mar14-hbs-2/">Comet Hale-Bopp</a> (ESO/E. Slawik, CC BY 4.0) and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1436a/">disc round HL Tauri</a> (ALMA (ESO/NAOJ/NRAO), CC BY 4.0); the <a href="https://esawebb.org/images/weic2332a/">rings of Uranus</a> (NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, CC BY 4.0); and from NASA, <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21736">Voyager 2 being tested</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22946">Jupiter from Juno</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS, processed by Kevin M. Gill, CC BY), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss059e027932">sunrise through the Earth’s air</a> (NASA), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA06193">Saturn in natural colour</a> and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21627">a wave in its rings</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10368">Phobos</a> and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA02308">Io</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24681">Ganymede</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/SwRI/MSSS) and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA01299">Jupiter’s four big moons</a> (NASA/JPL/DLR). The distances of the two Voyagers come from <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/">NASA’s JPL Horizons service</a>.</p>
        <p>Added to the older lessons on comets, asteroids, orbits and tides: <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/img_4937/">Comet McNaught from Paranal</a> (ESO/H. H. Heyer, CC BY 4.0); <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo22020a/">Comet Bernardinelli-Bernstein</a> (NASA, ESA, Man-To Hui, David Jewitt and Alyssa Pagan, CC BY 4.0) and <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic1814c/">Mars in 2018</a> (NASA, ESA and STScI, CC BY 4.0); and from NASA, <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA13602">Comet Hartley 2</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/UMD), a <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ202108110001">Perseid meteor</a> (NASA/Bill Ingalls), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/S85-39565">meteorite ALH84001</a> (NASA), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14894">Vesta</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/UCLA/MPS/DLR/IDA), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss071e700447">Comet Tsuchinshan-ATLAS from the International Space Station</a> (NASA), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000343">Mercury crossing the Sun</a> (NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center/SDO/Genna Duberstein), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000670">Moon crossing the Earth</a> (NASA/NOAA) and the <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA01912">Bay of Fundy at high and low tide</a> (NASA/GSFC/METI/ERSDAC/JAROS and the U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team). The tide chart is drawn from the forecasts of <a href="https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/stationhome.html?id=8410140">NOAA’s station at Eastport, Maine</a>.</p>
        <p>The <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/heic0406a/">Hubble Ultra Deep Field</a> is credited to NASA, ESA, S. Beckwith (STScI) and the HUDF Team and used under CC BY 4.0.</p>
        <p>The chart of planets round other stars in the lesson on <a href="/learn/the-kinds-of-planet-out-there">the kinds of planet out there</a> is drawn from the <a href="https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/">NASA Exoplanet Archive</a>, which is run by the California Institute of Technology under contract with NASA, as it stood on 11 November 2026.</p>
        <p>From NASA, which places no copyright on its own pictures: the <a href="https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA23645">Pale Blue Dot</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech), the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/nearside-spectacular">near</a> and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA14021">far</a> sides of the Moon (NASA/GSFC/Arizona State University), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss074e0208832">Meteor Crater from the International Space Station</a> (NASA), the <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/shaded-relief-with-height-as-color-yucatan-peninsula-mexico">height map of the Yucatán</a> (NASA/JPL), <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/darts-final-images-prior-to-impact/">DART’s last pictures of Dimorphos</a> (NASA/Johns Hopkins APL), <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/hubble-sees-boulders-escaping-from-asteroid-dimorphos/">Dimorphos and its tail from Hubble</a> (NASA, ESA and David Jewitt, UCLA; image processing by Alyssa Pagan, STScI), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/as17-148-22727">whole Earth from Apollo 17</a> and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss073e0247726">southern lights from the International Space Station</a> (NASA), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/as11-40-5903">Buzz Aldrin on the Moon</a> (NASA), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e001591">Earth at night</a> (NASA Earth Observatory/NOAA NGDC), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA19952">Pluto</a> and its moon <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA19968">Charon</a> (NASA/Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory/Southwest Research Institute), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA21906">Ceres</a> (NASA/JPL-Caltech/UCLA/MPS/DLR/IDA), the Sun’s <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000923">sunspots</a> and <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/GSFC_20171208_Archive_e000790">dark filament</a> (NASA/SDO), <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/as08-14-2383">Earthrise</a> from Apollo 8 (NASA / Bill Anders), the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/s132e013215">Space Station over the Earth</a> and the <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/iss017e019007">astronaut floating inside it</a>. All of these have been made smaller for the web and some are cropped. Where a mark or a label has been added, the words beside the picture say so.</p>
        <p>The two pictures of <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso2003c/">Betelgeuse before and during its dimming</a> are by ESO/M. Montargès et al., the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1313a/">stars circling over Paranal</a> are by ESO/F. Char, the Milky Way towards its centre in <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0934a/">ordinary light</a> (ESO/S. Guisard) and in <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1242a/">infrared</a> (ESO/VVV Survey/D. Minniti, with acknowledgement to Ignacio Toledo and Martin Kornmesser) and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0846a/">stars at the very centre</a> (ESO/S. Gillessen et al.) are from ESO, the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso1821a/">planet forming round PDS 70</a> is by ESO/A. Müller et al., the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/zodiacal_beletsky_potw/">zodiacal light over La Silla</a> is by ESO/Y. Beletsky, and the <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/eso0932a/">Milky Way panorama</a> is by ESO/S. Brunier. All are shared under the same Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence and have been made smaller for the web.</p>
        <p>The chart in the lesson on where the elements come from is our own drawing. Which element came from where is taken from <a href="https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/13873">Origins of the elements</a> by NASA’s Goddard Space Flight Center, which uses data gathered by Jennifer Johnson of Ohio State University.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry" id="beyond">
      <h2>Beyond the Solar System</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The <a href="/explore">3D view</a> can leave the planets behind and go out as far as the oldest light. What it shows on the way is built from four sets of measurements:</p>
        <ul class="drawn-list">
          <li><span>The stars</span> About 109,000 stars, each at its measured distance, from the <a href={HYG.url}>HYG star database, version 4.4</a>, put together by David Nash from the Hipparcos, Yale and Gliese catalogues. It is shared under a <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0</a> licence. Our star file, <a href="/data/deep/stars.bin">stars.bin</a>, is made from it and is shared under the same licence. It keeps each star’s place, brightness and colour and leaves out the stars whose distance is unknown or too great to trust.</li>
          <li><span>Nearby galaxies</span> About a hundred galaxies in and around the Local Group, with their places, distances and brightness, from <a href={MCCONNACHIE.url}>McConnachie 2012</a>, “The observed properties of dwarf galaxies in and around the Local Group”, Astronomical Journal 144, 4.</li>
          <li><span>The cosmic web</span> About 43,000 galaxies from the <a href={TWO_MRS.url}>2MASS Redshift Survey</a> (Huchra and others 2012, Astrophysical Journal Supplement 199, 26). Where each one is on the sky is measured. How far away it is has been worked out here from how fast it is moving away, taking 70 km a second for every megaparsec. Inside a cluster that smears galaxies into streaks pointing at us, which are not real.</li>
          <li><span>The oldest light</span> the nine-year map of the cosmic microwave background from NASA’s WMAP spacecraft (<a href={WMAP.url}>NASA / WMAP Science Team</a>).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The lines that join stars into constellation figures are not measurements. They are one modern convention for drawing the figures, taken from <a href={D3_CELESTIAL.url}>d3-celestial</a> by Olaf Frohn (copyright 2015, shared under the <a href="/data/deep/constellations.LICENSE.txt">BSD 3-Clause licence</a>). Each end of a line was matched here to a star in the catalogue above.</p>
        <p>Some of what you fly past out there is a model and not a map, because nobody has seen it from outside or, for the Oort cloud, at all:</p>
        <ul class="drawn-list">
          {places.filter((p) => p.model).map((p) => <li><span>{p.name}</span> {p.drawn}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>The Codex in the 3D view says the same thing at each of these places.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry">
      <h2>Saturn’s rings</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>The rings are drawn from the measured edges of each ring and gap in the <a href="https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/satringfact.html">NASA Saturnian Rings Fact Sheet</a>, from the faint D ring to the F ring, with each ring as see-through as it really is. The fine ringlets inside them are added by hand.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="entry drawn">
      <h2>What is drawn, not photographed</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>No spacecraft has stood where most of these pages take you, so the close-up scenes are generated as you watch. They follow what the missions saw and measured, but they are not photographs:</p>
        <ul class="drawn-list">
          <li><span>The Sun</span> granules at their real size of about 1,500 km, the supergranule network, a sunspot with its umbra, penumbra and the dip in the middle, and the darkening towards the edge of the disc.</li>
          <li><span>Mercury and Mars</span> the ground, rocks and small craters below the resolution of the maps.</li>
          <li><span>Venus</span> the surface under the cloud, modelled on the photographs the Venera landers sent back.</li>
          <li><span>Earth</span> the sea and clouds close up.</li>
          <li><span>Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune</span> the cloud decks and hazes, and where they sit relative to the Sun and moons.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="plates-sec" aria-labelledby="plates-h">
      <h2 id="plates-h">Every photograph</h2>
      <p class="plates-lead">{plates.length} pictures, all from NASA missions and photographers. NASA material is free to use for this kind of purpose, but NASA does not endorse this site.</p>
      <ol class="plates">
        {plates.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/${p.slug}`} class="plate-img"><img src={p.img} alt={p.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></a>
            <p class="plate-world">{p.world}</p>
            <p class="plate-cap">{p.caption}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="entry small">
      <h2>Type and code</h2>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>Each page has its own typeface, all free under the SIL Open Font Licence and served from this site through <a href="https://fontsource.org/">Fontsource</a>: Spectral, IBM Plex, Fraunces, Archivo, Bodoni Moda, Source Serif, Libre Caslon, Newsreader, Young Serif, Literata, Bevan, Libre Franklin, Italiana, EB Garamond, Instrument Serif, Big Shoulders and Inter. The site is built with <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> and draws in 3D with <a href="https://threejs.org/">three.js</a>, both under the MIT licence.</p>
        <p>Heliocodex is an independent project. It is not connected to NASA, JPL, Solar System Scope or any of the missions named here.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style is:global>
